WEBVTT

00:00:00.023 --> 00:00:05.972
NULLIF is especially useful when calculating ratios because division by zero is an error.

00:00:05.972 --> 00:00:11.037
Suppose we calculate percent paid as amount paid divided by bill amount.

00:00:11.037 --> 00:00:19.337
Most rows work normally, but one record has a bill amount of exactly zero because the service was categorized

00:00:19.337 --> 00:00:24.852
as No Charge. Dividing by zero does not make analytical sense, and PostgreSQL will not allow it.

00:00:24.852 --> 00:00:29.667
By using NULLIF on the denominator, we say: if bill amount equals zero,

00:00:29.667 --> 00:00:33.477
treat the denominator as NULL for this calculation.

00:00:33.477 --> 00:00:37.347
Division by NULL returns NULL instead of raising an error.

00:00:37.347 --> 00:00:44.247
That NULL result has a useful meaning: this percentage is not computable for a zero-dollar bill.

00:00:44.247 --> 00:00:47.667
It is not the same as zero percent paid.

00:00:47.667 --> 00:00:53.967
The numerator in the example may use COALESCE to treat missing amount paid as zero.

00:00:53.967 --> 00:00:56.747
Notice that this is a different decision.

00:00:56.747 --> 00:01:03.197
One function handles an invalid denominator; the other applies a fallback assumption to the numerator.

00:01:03.197 --> 00:01:09.572
This pattern appears in many domains: click-through rate, defect rate, utilization rate, conversion rate,

00:01:09.572 --> 00:01:14.082
mortality rate, or average cost per event.

00:01:14.082 --> 00:01:17.132
Any denominator can potentially be zero.

00:01:17.132 --> 00:01:20.372
Safe SQL does not merely prevent crashes.

00:01:20.372 --> 00:01:25.159
It preserves the meaning of the calculation while making exceptional cases visible.
